Subject: A contracted layout's chain is built once, outside any compiled frame

What this does
  - `qjs_aot_layout_build` is corrected: every property `add_property` creates gets
    its slot initialised (the call leaves it to the caller, and the temporary
    object's teardown frees every slot), the chain must end on a shape carrying
    every field, and the final shape is stamped while the object is still alive.
  - The unit builds Object.prototype layouts at the first compiled bind through
    the weak `qjs_aot_layouts_build`.
  - A layout whose instances come from a constructor is built when that
    constructor's function object exists. `js_aot_bind` marks the constructor's
    bytecode (`aot_layout_ctor`) when the weak `qjs_aot_layout_ctor` says a layout
    still waits on its digest, whether or not the unit compiled it. `js_closure`
    (now a thin wrapper of `js_closure_impl`) and the end of `js_op_define_class`
    call the weak `qjs_aot_layout_fn_created` for a marked function, after the
    closure is complete and, for a class, after its `.prototype` is defined. The
    mark is dropped once the unit has nothing left to build for that digest.
  - `qjs_aot_function_prototype` reads the constructor's own plain data
    `.prototype`, forcing the automatic prototype and never running a getter.
    `qjs_aot_object_prototype` gives the unit Object.prototype.
  - A failure inside the hook leaves no pending exception.

Why we need it
  Laying out a chain allocates, and an allocation inside a compiled body is a
  safepoint the frame is not prepared for. Bind runs before any function object
  exists, so a constructor's layout can only be built when its function object is
  made. Building at those two points keeps every allocation on the interpreter's
  stack.

Hazards
  The hook runs inside the closure helper, which compiled code reaches through the
  interpreter's make_closure handler; that handler already allocates there, so the
  frame is already visible to the collector. A layout whose prototype is missing,
  an accessor, or not an object is refused for good and nothing is stamped, so
  values of that class simply fail the layout check and take the generic body.

Functions touched
  `js_closure`, `js_closure_impl`, `js_op_define_class`, `js_aot_bind`,
  `js_aot_layout_fn_hook` (new), `qjs_aot_layout_build`,
  `qjs_aot_object_prototype` (new), `qjs_aot_function_prototype` (new);
  `JSFunctionBytecode` gains the `aot_layout_ctor` byte and `JSRuntime` the flag
  `aot_layouts_built`.

No (nothing is built until a unit declares a layout).

